1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,240
How do you know which network a card belongs to before you ask anyone?

2
00:00:03,290 --> 00:00:09,408
In Lesson 3, the masked number kept the first six digits, because they tell which bank gave out the card.

3
00:00:09,458 --> 00:00:12,240
In Lesson 9, the full number was sealed away.

4
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:17,856
This lesson shows what those first digits tell the card zone, without opening the full number.

5
00:00:17,906 --> 00:00:19,728
Think of a phone number.

6
00:00:19,778 --> 00:00:22,032
Its first digits tell you the country.

7
00:00:22,082 --> 00:00:24,504
They tell you that before anyone answers.

8
00:00:24,554 --> 00:00:26,448
They never tell you who you are calling.

9
00:00:26,498 --> 00:00:30,024
And a longer code is more exact than a shorter one.

10
00:00:30,074 --> 00:00:31,896
A card number works the same way.

11
00:00:31,946 --> 00:00:35,808
Its first digits name the card network and the bank that issued the card.

12
00:00:35,858 --> 00:00:40,464
Those first digits are called the BIN, short for bank identification number.

13
00:00:40,514 --> 00:00:46,560
Each network publishes its own list of BINs: which first digits belong to which bank.

14
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:49,152
That is the network's BIN list.

15
00:00:49,202 --> 00:00:52,728
The card zone copies those lists in through the adapters.

16
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:56,952
It copies them when it starts, and again on the bank's chosen schedule.

17
00:00:57,002 --> 00:01:01,152
Sometimes a network does not answer, or answers with an empty list.

18
00:01:01,202 --> 00:01:03,600
Then the card zone keeps its old list.

19
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:05,952
An old list is better than no list.

20
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:07,824
A lookup works like a phone code.

21
00:01:07,874 --> 00:01:10,488
The card zone takes the first eight digits.

22
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:13,728
It tries all eight, then seven, then six.

23
00:01:13,778 --> 00:01:17,280
The longest match wins, because it is the most exact.

24
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:22,368
The lookup runs in memory, so it is fast and never touches the database.

25
00:01:22,418 --> 00:01:26,736
And the part that knows BINs only ever sees those first eight digits.

26
00:01:26,786 --> 00:01:28,608
It never sees the whole number.

27
00:01:28,658 --> 00:01:31,680
An unknown BIN is not an error.

28
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:33,864
The answer is simply an empty list.

29
00:01:33,914 --> 00:01:35,736
Why does this matter?

30
00:01:35,786 --> 00:01:42,024
Before any network is asked to send a code to a buyer's phone, the card zone checks the card's network this way.

31
00:01:42,074 --> 00:01:44,400
A shop may accept only some networks.

32
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:49,488
If the card belongs to a network the shop does not take, the card is refused right there.

33
00:01:49,538 --> 00:01:51,480
No network is bothered.

34
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:53,352
No phone buzzes.

35
00:01:53,402 --> 00:01:55,224
Now watch the product.

36
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:57,648
The buyer opens the bank's payment page.

37
00:01:57,698 --> 00:02:01,800
Above the card form, the page shows the networks this shop accepts.

38
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:05,616
The buyer types a card from another network and presses "Send code".

39
00:02:05,666 --> 00:02:09,264
The form answers at once: "This shop doesn't accept this card.

40
00:02:09,314 --> 00:02:11,880
Use another card." No code is sent.

41
00:02:11,930 --> 00:02:13,752
Nobody's phone lights up.

42
00:02:13,802 --> 00:02:18,696
Next, the buyer types a card whose first digits no network has listed.

43
00:02:18,746 --> 00:02:22,752
The form says that no network issues cards starting with those digits.

44
00:02:22,802 --> 00:02:24,624
Again, nothing is sent.

45
00:02:24,674 --> 00:02:27,336
Then the buyer types a card the shop accepts.

46
00:02:27,386 --> 00:02:29,208
A code arrives on the buyer's phone.

47
00:02:29,258 --> 00:02:31,416
The buyer types it and confirms.

48
00:02:31,466 --> 00:02:33,288
The form says "Card accepted".

49
00:02:33,338 --> 00:02:38,568
The payment page now shows the masked number, the card's network, and the name of the bank that issued it.

50
00:02:38,618 --> 00:02:41,184
That bank name came from the first digits alone.

51
00:02:41,234 --> 00:02:44,136
On the bank's side, nothing alarming happens.

52
00:02:44,186 --> 00:02:48,096
A wrong-network card is a normal refusal, not an incident.

53
00:02:48,146 --> 00:02:51,312
The team only hears about it if the lists stop arriving.

54
00:02:51,362 --> 00:02:53,184
What does this protect?

55
00:02:53,234 --> 00:02:57,120
Without the check, the card zone would ask the wrong centre to send a code.

56
00:02:57,170 --> 00:03:00,864
A real person's phone would buzz for a payment that can never work.

57
00:03:00,914 --> 00:03:04,008
The network would carry a request it should never see.

58
00:03:04,058 --> 00:03:07,344
With the check, a wrong card stops at its first digits.

59
00:03:07,394 --> 00:03:09,768
It also protects the number itself.

60
00:03:09,818 --> 00:03:14,616
The part that names networks needs only the first eight digits, so that is all it gets.

61
00:03:14,666 --> 00:03:16,488
Back to the phone.

62
00:03:16,538 --> 00:03:21,744
A country code tells you where a call goes, before anyone answers, and never who picks up.

63
00:03:21,794 --> 00:03:24,024
A card's first digits do the same.

64
00:03:24,074 --> 00:03:27,360
They name the network and the bank, never the person.

65
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:31,560
Next: how the card form protects itself in the buyer's browser.
